  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    bg = grid[0][0]\n    \n    # Find all non-background cells\n    points = {}\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] != bg:\n                points.setdefault(grid[r][c], []).append((r, c))\n    \n    # Find rectangular patterns (4 corners of same color)\n    rects = []\n    for color, pts in points.items():\n        if len(pts) == 4:\n            rs = sorted(set(r for r, c in pts))\n            cs = sorted(set(c for r, c in pts))\n            if len(rs) == 2 and len(cs) == 2:\n                rects.append((color, rs[0], cs[0], rs[1], cs[1]))\n    \n    # Also handle 2-point patterns (lines)\n    lines = []\n    for color, pts in points.items():\n        if len(pts) == 2:\n            lines.append((color, pts))\n    \n    # Find the largest rect to use as output frame, then overlay others\n    # Actually, let me reconsider: we need to find overlapping rectangles and merge them\n    \n    # All rects define the same-sized output grid when normalized\n    # Find the output size from the largest bounding box\n    all_non_bg = [(r, c, grid[r][c]) for r in range(rows) for c in range(cols) if grid[r][c] != bg]\n    \n    # Group into clusters that share rows/cols... \n    # Simpler: each rect/line defines corners/edges. The output is formed by superimposing all patterns\n    # onto a common grid defined by the union of their relative positions.\n    \n    # Each pattern has a center or reference. Let me find overlapping anchor points.\n    # Looking at examples: patterns share some row/col coordinates and get merged.\n    \n    # Collect all unique rows and cols with non-bg values\n    all_rs = sorted(set(r for r, c, v in all_non_bg))\n    all_cs = sorted(set(c for r, c, v in all_non_bg))\n    \n    # Map rows and cols to indices\n    r_map = {r: i for i, r in enumerate(all_rs)}\n    c_map = {c: i for i, c in enumerate(all_cs)}\n    \n    out_rows = len(all_rs)\n    out_cols = len(all_cs)\n    out = [[bg] * out_cols for _ in range(out_rows)]\n    \n    for r, c, v in all_non_bg:\n        out[r_map[r]][c_map[c]] = v\n    \n    return out",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    bg = grid[0][0]\n    \n    # Find all non-background cells\n    points = {}\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] != bg:\n                points.setdefault(grid[r][c], []).append((r, c))\n    \n    # Find rectangular patterns (4 corners of same color)\n    rects = []\n    for color, pts in points.items():\n        if len(pts) == 4:\n            rs = sorted(set(r for r, c in pts))\n            cs = sorted(set(c for r, c in pts))\n            if len(rs) == 2 and len(cs) == 2:\n                rects.append((color, rs[0], cs[0], rs[1], cs[1]))\n    \n    # Also handle 2-point patterns (lines)\n    lines = []\n    for color, pts in points.items():\n        if len(pts) == 2:\n            lines.append((color, pts))\n    \n    # Find the largest rect to use as output frame, then overlay others\n    # Actually, let me reconsider: we need to find overlapping rectangles and merge them\n    \n    # All rects define the same-sized output grid when normalized\n    # Find the output size from the largest bounding box\n    all_non_bg = [(r, c, grid[r][c]) for r in range(rows) for c in range(cols) if grid[r][c] != bg]\n    \n    # Group into clusters that share rows/cols... \n    # Simpler: each rect/line defines corners/edges. The output is formed by superimposing all patterns\n    # onto a common grid defined by the union of their relative positions.\n    \n    # Each pattern has a center or reference. Let me find overlapping anchor points.\n    # Looking at examples: patterns share some row/col coordinates and get merged.\n    \n    # Collect all unique rows and cols with non-bg values\n    all_rs = sorted(set(r for r, c, v in all_non_bg))\n    all_cs = sorted(set(c for r, c, v in all_non_bg))\n    \n    # Map rows and cols to indices\n    r_map = {r: i for i, r in enumerate(all_rs)}\n    c_map = {c: i for i, c in enumerate(all_cs)}\n    \n    out_rows = len(all_rs)\n    out_cols = len(all_cs)\n    out = [[bg] * out_cols for _ in range(out_rows)]\n    \n    for r, c, v in all_non_bg:\n        out[r_map[r]][c_map[c]] = v\n    \n    return out",
